WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:06.297
- Yo, Yelo, what up?

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:08.258
- Yo, Chris, you see that video I just sent you?

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:09.592
- Yeah, man, I just seen it 

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:11.344
Who is that? He seem like he nice

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:12.303
- Trashaun Willis

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:13.638
That kid can shoot, man

00:00:13.888 --> 00:00:15.265
I think I might need you to go out there and check him out

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:16.558
- I’m in, I’m in

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.351
- Aight, we about to make this kid’s day

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:21.479
- Yo C, can we stop in Iowa real quick?

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:31.072
[“Dedication” by Nipsey Hussle (feat. Kendrick Lamar) plays]

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:42.500
- Out here in the middle of Iowa, cornfield on cornfield on cornfield

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.419
It’s the home of Trashaun Willis

00:00:44.919 --> 00:00:47.964
A natural athlete born with only one arm

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.050
He went viral for dunking it

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:52.427
And he plays QB

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.681
Don’t tell him he’s “disabled,” and do not call him “special”

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:58.975
He’s only a sophomore in high school

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.061
And this kid is just getting started

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:06.316
- The first one that got caught on camera was at the middle school

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.820
It was, like, third or fourth quarter, and I stole it and then I dunked it

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.156
And then you just see the student section going crazy

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.162
- So I just asked the dean of students for the security footage

00:01:19.162 --> 00:01:21.998
And then I screen-recorded it and posted it on Twitter

00:01:21.998 --> 00:01:24.793
- People were just, like, just, I don’t know, it just blew up

00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:27.879
- All my friends were, like, boosting me up and stuff 

00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:31.382
And saying, like, “Oh, you’re famous, blah, blah, blah”

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.512
It’s just natural to me, to just go out and do my best

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:38.932
And I guess that just amazes people because I do have one arm

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:47.357
- Trashaun was born with amniotic band syndrome

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:52.862
Which is a band in your amniotic fluid that wraps around something that’s developing

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.073
You go through a hundred different emotions

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:58.952
Of, “How is my child going to survive with one arm?”

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:02.122
But there was nothing that he couldn’t do or learn how to do

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:05.416
He was able to learn to ride a bike, zip and button his pants

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.253
So when Trashaun’s dunk went viral

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.298
I guess it didn’t really surprise me that he could dunk a basketball

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:16.344
- For us to put limitations on Trashaun clearly isn’t fair

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:18.054
I mean, he’s still a big 6’4”-bodied kid 

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:20.723
With an incredibly strong lower body that’s got a great vertical jump

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.518
- You’re scared, you’re scared, huh?

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:25.812
- He’s out there playing with one hand, and he’s dominating

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.189
I mean, there’s times last year I couldn’t take him off the court

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:29.691
Because he was the best player on our team

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:31.568
He’s really good with his outside shot

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:36.072
- My jump shot, I think it’s normal, so I just take my hand and put it on the back

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:39.200
And then I just use my little arm to make sure it doesn’t really fall off

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.201
So that’s just what I do

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:43.955
The only adjustments I’d say are, like, dribbling

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.291
Most teams try and force me to go to my left

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.252
- He’s got, like, this kind of goofy Eurostep that he uses

00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:53.548
He can’t crossover. He just can’t do it. So he has a move

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:56.885
Where he just goes behind his back and then catches it on the left side

00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:59.762
- It’s times when he was constantly getting the ball stolen from him

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:01.848
He’s trying to figure it out and figure it out

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:03.808
And he just couldn’t

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:06.936
He couldn’t just figure it out, If he just made that one little turn

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.814
And turned like this with the ball, he’d just start working it out

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:12.483
Kids come at him, kids really don’t care 

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:13.860
They’re competitors as well, you know?

00:03:14.235 --> 00:03:16.571
- You know, they’re getting scored on by a kid that only has one arm

00:03:16.905 --> 00:03:17.947
They don’t like it

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.533
- The one game last year, some kid

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.369
I swatted him, and it went out of bounds, and I said, “Get that out of here!”

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.288
He’s like, “Shut up, you literally have one arm” 

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.249
So that just set me off, I went off on him

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:32.795
- He never comes to me and says, “Dad, I can’t get through this”

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:36.341
He admits that stuff is hard, you know, but he never says that he can’t

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:40.220
Usually people that got some kind of,...we give them a pass on “I can’t”

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.431
Because we have compassion for them, but I can’t with my son

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:45.934
We look for a victory this week

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.811
You know your friends keep coming up to me and say they want you to

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:50.855
They want to still see you at quarterback

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:54.859
- Varsity guys, we need to be ready for practice

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.987
Hey, we gotta have a good one, we gotta have a good one today

00:03:57.987 --> 00:03:59.530
We’re going to right this ship, alright?

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:01.991
[Students cheer]

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:06.204
- Trashaun is our varsity starting middle linebacker, No. 2 backup QB

00:04:06.204 --> 00:04:08.581
He’s really the leader and quarterback of our defense

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:13.419
He’s just a no-excuses kid that always shows up

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.588
And gets it done in spite of his handicap

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.924
I would consider him to have a physical handicap

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.592
I would not consider him disabled because

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:22.720
He is more able than many of the players we have on our team with both arms

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:27.684
- So I go to a second-grade classroom

00:04:27.684 --> 00:04:32.438
And I just help kids out with either their math

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.399
or reading or setting up their portfolio

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.733
Whatever they need help with

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:38.236
So 10, 20, what comes after 20?

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:38.987
- 30?

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:39.404
- There you go

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.616
- Trashaun’s awesome, the kids love to have him in our classroom

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.452
They love to have him out on the playground, and they look up to him, you know?

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.413
He’s a football player, he’s a basketball player

00:04:50.039 --> 00:04:53.334
I had Trashaun in second grade, and there was one thing that Trashaun told me

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:56.754
That he couldn’t do and that was tie his shoes, and that was the only thing

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.549
- That was pretty hard, I didn’t learn that until, like, second grade

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:03.261
But even then, like, that’s still good for me

00:05:03.594 --> 00:05:05.638
- I remember the first time I noticed he tied his shoes

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.223
They were tied, he had did it

00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:10.351
When we tried to reach down and tie his shoes, he was, like, not having it

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:13.521
So he was going to be OK when he stepped out into school and the world

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:19.736
- Trashaun is living his life the only way he’s ever known, but let’s be real

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:23.364
One arm or two, he’s still got to prove himself every day

00:05:23.364 --> 00:05:24.824
On the court, and off

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:28.703
Going viral was legit, but going pro is the focus

00:05:29.454 --> 00:05:33.750
- I hope someday that I can play at the highest level, the NBA or football

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.210
That’s my ultimate goal, all the way up there

00:05:36.586 --> 00:05:40.715
Ever since, like, last football season, I’ve just been looking up to Shaquem Griffin

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.051
He has what I have, amniotic band syndrome

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:44.510
Anything is possible, I guess

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.554
Like, if he can do it, anyone can do it

00:05:46.721 --> 00:05:47.722
I can do it

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:57.982
- For me, you know, playing basketball in the back yard, that was my passion

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:01.527
You know, when I finished my homework, me and my brother went outside

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:05.239
We would play until my mom said it was time to come in and eat dinner

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:10.119
You know, everyone has their own story, you know, and you really don't know

00:06:10.119 --> 00:06:14.499
Where somebody’s from until you really go visit them and see what it’s like

00:06:15.792 --> 00:06:19.879
For me, it’s just going and basically putting a smile on his face

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:23.049
And let him know that somebody will come to Iowa

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:27.845
You know, and drive past all these cornfields, and give him the time

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.561
- His dad know?

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:37.855
Oh, I know

00:06:43.152 --> 00:06:43.945
- Whoa, who’s that?

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:45.988
Oh, that’s Chris Paul

00:06:45.988 --> 00:06:47.281
Oh, hecks no

00:06:47.281 --> 00:06:47.824
- CP!

00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:48.783
